{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 45, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Tripoli and the last ruler of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state established in the Levant during the 12th century. He was born in 1140 and died in 1187.\n\n* **Early Life** *\nRaymond III was the son of Bohemond III King of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and his third wife Orgueilleuse of the Hauteville family. His early life is not well documented however it is known that he was educated in the Latin East and was a skilled knight.\n\n* **Succession** *\nIn 1152 Raymond III succeeded his father as King of Jerusalem however he did not remain king for long as he was forced to abdicate in 1162 in favor of his nephew Amalric I. He then went on to rule the County of Tripoli which he inherited from his father.\n\nRaymond III was a skilled military leader and played a key role in the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where he led the vanguard of the Crusader army. He also played a key role in the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where he was taken prisoner by the Ayyubid forces.\n\n* **Personal Life** *\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Armenia in 1160 however the marriage was childless. He then married a woman named Alice of Jerusalem however the marriage was also childless. It is believed that he may have had a son named Bohemond but this is not confirmed.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin. He was taken prisoner by the Ayyubid forces and was later executed. The County of Tripoli was then absorbed into the Ayyubid Empire.\n\nThe legacy of Raymond III is complex and multifaceted. He was a skilled military leader and a key player in the politics of the Latin East however his failure to produce an heir led to the eventual collapse of the County of Tripoli. Despite this his bravery and military prowess have made him a legendary figure in the history of the Crusades.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", 